## Service 1 — Token Service

**What this service does in one sentence:** When a user wants to go offline, this service contacts the ledger service to lock their funds, then generates a signed JWT token that acts as their digital "permission slip" to spend up to a certain amount while offline.

```
token-service/
├── controller/
│   └── TokenController.java
├── service/
│   └── TokenService.java
├── model/
│   └── TokenRequest.java
│   └── TokenResponse.java
│   └── TokenRecord.java
├── repository/
│   └── TokenRepository.java
├── security/
│   └── JwtUtil.java
└── config/
    └── SecurityConfig.java
```

Here is what each package does:

**`controller/`** — This is the door to your service. It receives HTTP requests from the outside world and sends back responses. Think of it as a receptionist. It does zero business logic — it just receives, passes to service, and returns the answer.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/tokens")
public class TokenController {

    @Autowired
    private TokenService tokenService;

    // User calls this before going offline
    @PostMapping("/request")
    public ResponseEntity<TokenResponse> requestToken(@RequestBody TokenRequest request) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(tokenService.issueToken(request));
    }

    // Merchant calls this to verify a token is valid
    @PostMapping("/validate")
    public ResponseEntity<Boolean> validateToken(@RequestBody String token) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(tokenService.validateToken(token));
    }
}
```

**`service/`** — This is where all the actual thinking happens. The controller hands the request to the service, which decides what to do. The service calls the ledger service to reserve funds, then calls JwtUtil to create the token, then saves a record of it to the database.

**`model/`** — These are just data containers. `TokenRequest` holds what the user sends (userId, amount, hours). `TokenResponse` holds what you send back (the JWT string, expiry time). `TokenRecord` is the JPA entity — this is what gets saved to the Supabase database as a row in the `tokens` table.

**`repository/`** — This is the database layer. Spring Data JPA generates all the SQL for you automatically. You just declare what you need:

```java
public interface TokenRepository extends JpaRepository<TokenRecord, UUID> {
    Optional<TokenRecord> findByUserId(String userId);
    List<TokenRecord> findByStatus(String status);
}
```

**`security/`** — `JwtUtil` handles signing and verifying JWTs. It uses a secret key stored in your environment variables (never in code). It creates tokens with claims like `userId`, `maxAmount`, `spentAmount`, `expiresAt`.

**`config/`** — `SecurityConfig` tells Spring Security which endpoints need authentication and which are public. The `/validate` endpoint is called by merchants so it needs to be accessible.

