## Service 4 — Fraud Service

**What this service does in one sentence:** Before any offline transaction gets settled, this service looks at it and gives it a risk score — flagging suspicious patterns like spending ₹2000 in 10 transactions within 30 seconds, or transactions happening at 3am when the user has never transacted at that hour.

```
fraud-service/
├── controller/
│   └── FraudController.java
├── service/
│   └── FraudScoringService.java
├── rules/
│   └── VelocityRule.java
│   └── AmountAnomalyRule.java
│   └── TimePatternRule.java
│   └── RuleEngine.java
├── model/
│   └── FraudCheckRequest.java
│   └── FraudCheckResponse.java
│   └── FraudRecord.java
└── repository/
    └── FraudRepository.java
```

**`controller/`** — One endpoint: `POST /check` which receives a transaction and returns `{"score": 0.82, "decision": "REJECT", "reason": "VELOCITY_BREACH"}`.

**`rules/`** — This is the most interesting package. Each rule is a separate class implementing a `Rule` interface. `VelocityRule` checks: did this user make more than 5 transactions in the last 60 seconds? `AmountAnomalyRule` checks: is this amount more than 3x the user's average transaction? `TimePatternRule` checks: has this user ever transacted at this hour before? `RuleEngine` runs all rules and combines their scores into a final number between 0 and 1.

This design — called the Strategy Pattern — means you can add new fraud rules without touching existing code. That is a very impressive thing to mention in a technical interview.

