## Part 2 — The Problem, From Scratch

Imagine you are a vegetable seller in a village in Kerala. You have a UPI QR code. A flood hits. Internet goes down for 3 days. You cannot sell anything. Your customer has ₹500 in their phone but cannot pay you. You both have the money and the willingness — but the system fails you.

This is not rare. This happens during:
- Cyclones, floods, earthquakes (India has all three regularly)
- Telecom tower outages
- Remote tribal or hilly areas with poor signal
- Power cuts that take down local cell towers

The problem isn't that people don't have money. The problem is that the system that proves they have money requires the internet to be alive at all times.

Your project solves this. Here is how, in plain English:

> Before going offline, your app talks to the bank and says: "Lock ₹500 from this person's account. Give them a secure digital proof they can spend up to ₹500 while offline." When internet returns, all the transactions that happened offline are sent to the bank and settled properly.

The money never moves unsafely. The bank always stays in control. But the person can pay even when offline.

